        </div>
        <div class="msv__step">
          <div class="msv__step-num">02</div>
          <div class="msv__step-body">
            <h3>Apostille the FBI Check via US State Department</h3>
            <p>Mail your FBI background check to the US State Department Authentications Office in Washington D.C. for an apostille. Standard processing takes 6–8 weeks; expedited services are available for an additional fee. The State Department — not New York State — issues apostilles on federal documents.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__step">
          <div class="msv__step-num">03</div>
          <div class="msv__step-body">
            <h3>Get Your Medical Certificate</h3>
            <p>Any licensed physician in the US can complete your NLV medical certificate. It confirms you have no communicable diseases that pose a public health risk. The certificate must be signed, dated, and translated into Spanish by a certified translator. It is valid for 90 days — time it to align with your appointment date.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__step">
          <div class="msv__step-num">04</div>
          <div class="msv__step-body">
            <h3>Obtain Private Health Insurance</h3>
            <p>Purchase a Spanish-registered private health insurance policy with no copays and no exclusions for pre-existing conditions (for the duration of the visa). Popular providers for US applicants include established private health insurers in Spain leading private insurer. Medicare does not satisfy this requirement.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__step">
          <div class="msv__step-num">05</div>
          <div class="msv__step-body">
            <h3>Prepare Your Financial Documentation</h3>
            <p>Gather 6 months of bank statements from all accounts, income award letters (Social Security, pension), most recent tax returns (Form 1040), brokerage statements, or any other documentation showing your income sources. Have a certified Spanish translation prepared for all documents.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__step">
          <div class="msv__step-num">06</div>
          <div class="msv__step-body">
            <h3>Complete the Visa Application Form (EX-01)</h3>
            <p>Download and complete Spain's national long-stay visa application form (Form EX-01). The form must be completed in Spanish or with a certified translation. Two original signed copies are typically required. Do not date the form more than a few weeks before your appointment.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__step">
          <div class="msv__step-num">07</div>
          <div class="msv__step-body">
            <h3>Attend Your In-Person Consulate Appointment</h3>
            <p>Appear in person at the Consulate General of Spain, 150 E 58th St, New York, NY 10155. Bring all original documents plus copies. Pay the consulate visa fee (approximately €80–€100 per applicant, typically payable by money order). Biometrics may be taken at this appointment.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__step">
          <div class="msv__step-num">08</div>
          <div class="msv__step-body">
            <h3>Wait for Decision &amp; Collect Your Visa</h3>
            <p>Processing takes 10–16 weeks. You'll be notified when a decision is made. If approved, collect your visa in person or arrange for collection via an authorized representative. Once issued, travel to Spain within the validity period and register with the local Foreigners' Office (Oficina de Extranjeros) within 30 days.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Document Checklist ────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section--cream">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>Document Checklist for New York Applicants</h2>
        <p>Prepare originals and photocopies of all documents. Translated documents must be certified. Check the consulate's current requirements at the time of your application as lists can change.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__two-col">
        <div class="msv__checklist">
          <h3>Identity &amp; Legal Documents</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Valid US passport (6+ months remaining, 2 blank pages)</li>
            <li>Passport photos (US standard 2"×2", white background)</li>
            <li>Completed Form EX-01 (2 copies)</li>
            <li>FBI Identity History Summary + State Department apostille</li>
            <li>Certified Spanish translation of FBI check</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__checklist">
          <h3>Health &amp; Insurance</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Medical certificate from licensed US physician</li>
            <li>Certified Spanish translation of medical certificate</li>
            <li>Private health insurance policy (Spain-registered insurer)</li>
            <li>Insurance must show no copays, no coverage gaps</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__checklist">
          <h3>Financial Documents</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>6 months of bank statements (all accounts)</li>
            <li>Social Security benefit verification letter (if applicable)</li>
            <li>Pension award letter (if applicable)</li>
            <li>401(k) / IRA distribution schedule or statements</li>
            <li>Most recent US tax return (Form 1040)</li>
            <li>Brokerage account statements (if using investment income)</li>
            <li>Certified Spanish translations of key financial docs</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__checklist">
          <h3>Additional Items</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Proof of accommodation in Spain (lease, property deed, hotel letter)</li>
            <li>Visa fee payment (money order, approx €80–€100)</li>
            <li>Self-addressed return envelope (if submitting by mail — confirm with consulate)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--warn" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
        <div class="msv__alert-icon">⚠️</div>
        <p><strong>Always verify the current checklist</strong> directly with the Consulate General of Spain in New York before your appointment. Requirements can be updated, and individual consulates may have slightly different document preferences. Our specialist can help you prepare a complete, consulate-specific file.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── FAQ ───────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions — New York Applicants</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__faq-list">
        <details>
          <summary>Do I have to use the New York consulate if I live in New Jersey or Connecticut?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes. The Consulate General of Spain in New York has jurisdiction over New York, New Jersey, Connecticut, Pennsylvania, Massachusetts, Maine, New Hampshire, Vermont, and Rhode Island. Regardless of which state within this region you live in, this is your consulate. You cannot choose a different Spanish consulate in the US to avoid wait times or for any other reason — your application will be rejected if submitted outside your jurisdiction.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I get my FBI background check apostilled in New York?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>No. The FBI background check is a federal document and must be apostilled by the US State Department in Washington D.C. — not by the New York Secretary of State. New York can apostille state-issued documents (like state police checks, birth certificates, and court documents), but the FBI check requires the federal apostille authority.</p>
            <p>You can mail your FBI check directly to the US State Department Authentications Office, or use an authorized apostille service to handle the process. Allow 6–8 weeks for standard State Department processing, or use an expedited service if your timeline is tight.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does Social Security income count toward the NLV requirement?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes, absolutely. US Social Security benefits are fully recognized as passive income for the Non-Lucrative Visa. To document it, you'll need your most recent Social Security Benefit Verification Letter (available free at ssa.gov or your local SSA office) plus 3–6 months of bank statements confirming the deposits.</p>
            <p>For 2026, a single applicant needs approximately $2,600/month. Many New York retirees with Social Security plus investment income or a pension easily meet this threshold.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I use 401(k) or IRA distributions as income proof?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes, provided the distributions are regular and recurring. The consulate is looking for a consistent monthly income, not a lump sum withdrawal. You'll need to show your distribution schedule or account statements demonstrating regular monthly withdrawals, along with bank statements confirming those deposits are arriving each month.</p>
            <p>If you haven't started taking distributions yet but are of RMD age or plan to set up regular withdrawals, a letter from your financial institution confirming the distribution amount and frequency can help support your application.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How far in advance should I start preparing my New York NLV application?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Start at least 6–9 months before your intended move date. The FBI background check alone — including the apostille — can take 10–16 weeks depending on which route you use. Add 4–8 weeks to secure a consulate appointment, then 10–16 weeks for consulate processing. Your medical certificate is only valid for 90 days, so time it to be completed no more than 3 months before your appointment.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do US passport photos work for the Spanish consulate application?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes. Standard US passport photos (2"×2", white or off-white background, taken within the last 6 months) are acceptable for the Spanish NLV application at the New York consulate. Make sure they are recent and clearly show your full face without glasses or head coverings (unless for religious reasons).</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

